// LifecycleState A DEVICE'S PLACE IN ITS LIFE — "a small state machine: ORDERED → ENROLLED → ASSIGNED → IN
// REPAIR → LOST → RETIRED → RECYCLED". The seven are one set, so all are named.
//
// SIX CAN BE ANSWERED TODAY. `enrolled` — in the register with no accepted holder: nobody
// is named, or a hand-over waits to be accepted. `assigned` — somebody holds it: a
// company-owned or shared device once a hand-over form is accepted; a person's own device,
// and every device of a personal account environment, from the moment it enrols.
// `in_repair` — away, and coming back. `lost` — in lost mode (ONPLO-2482). `retired` — the
// device answered its un-enrol, and its receipt is kept (ONPLO-2666). Retired comes before
// every other state: retiring ends a repair or lost mode the device was in. `recycled` — a
// retired device whose disposal is recorded (`recycleDevice`, ONPLO-1510); it comes before
// retired, and keeps its retirement receipt.
//
// THE OTHER ONE IS NAMED AND NEVER ANSWERED YET, until its move is declared: `ordered` (a
// record made before the hardware exists). (Until ONPLO-2666 this said "FOUR CAN BE
// ANSWERED TODAY" and named `retired` among the three never answered; until ONPLO-1510 it
// said "FIVE" and named `recycled` beside `ordered`. A wipe will retire a device too, when it
// is declared.)
type LifecycleState string

