	//
	// THE CERTIFICATE REQUEST IS CARRIED AND NEVER READ. Whether it is well formed, signed by its
	// own key and for a P-256 key, the one kind identity certifies (decision 0963), is judged where
	// the certificate is issued. Each refusal comes back as a 422 at `/body/certificate_request`,
	// and its entry's `code` says which, in identity's own words: `certificate_request_unreadable`,
	// `certificate_request_not_signed_by_its_key`, `key_not_certified`, or `key_ended` — a key
	// whose certificate was ended is never certified again, so the device makes a new pair.
	//
	// IDENTITY IS TOLD FIRST, THEN ASKED FOR THE CERTIFICATE, and only then is the device's record
	// written: the device exists for identity before anybody can be granted anything on it. When
	// identity cannot be reached, nothing is written and the same request can be sent again; the
	// device's id is the same on every try, so a retry finishes what the first began.
	//
	// THE DEVICE READS `unknown` UNTIL ITS FIRST CHECK-IN, and its reading then names its source
	// as `device_agent`.
	//
	// A SERVER'S ENROLMENT IS NOT FINISHED HERE. It is refused `enrolment_door_mismatch`: its
	// code finishes it, with nobody signed in.
	//
	// WHERE THE DEVICE LANDS. In the group the door it passes hands out, and following its setup: your
	// own device passes the Your own device door, a company-owned or shared computer the by-hand door.
	// Identity is told the device joined the group after the certificate, and the device follows the
	// setup by itself, at its latest version, enforced. A setup that does not fit a computer is not
	// applied, and the device follows what it would. A door that names neither hands out the account
