// ListDevices The devices you may see
//
// ONE LIST, EVERY KIND. A computer, a phone, a server and a printer sit in one register. The
// kind is a property of the row and a filter here, never a second list.
//
// WORST FIRST, because this is a work queue: a device that fails a rule comes before one
// that passes. The order is each device's own reading, in four steps: `not_compliant`, then
// `unknown`, then `stale`, then `compliant`. Inside one step the device added first comes
// first.
//
// AN EXEMPT DEVICE SITS WHERE ITS OWN READING PUTS IT. The exemption is the Compliance app's,
// laid over a page once the page is chosen. So a device excused from a failing rule is still
// among the first rows, reading `exempt` with its reason.
//
// THE ORDER IS WORKED OUT WHEN A PAGE IS READ, NEVER STORED. A reading goes stale and an
// update passes its deadline while nothing happens, so a stored order would be wrong the
// moment either did. A device whose word moves between two pages may be met twice or not at
// all; the list read again from its start is whole.
//
// A ROW CARRIES NO GROUPS (decision 0768). The register's row shows none, and Group is a
// filter here. The groups a device is in are read on the device itself (`getDevice`).
//
// IT ANSWERS IN THE ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT THIS CREDENTIAL ACTS IN and never beyond it.
//
// EACH ROW CARRIES ITS HEALTH READING. That is how the Compliance app fills its roster: it
// reads the word, the failing rule, the serial and the moment of the reading from here.
//
// THE FILTERS ARE THE CHIPS THE PAGE SHOWS, less Setup and Managed elsewhere, which this list
// does not narrow by yet.
//
// BEHIND ON UPDATES IS A FILTER (ONPLO-2493): `behind_on_updates=true` answers the devices with
// an update offered and not installed. That is every device on a schedule's four counts, so it
// is wider than `devices_behind_count`, which leaves out one that is postponing, past its
// deadline or failing. Nothing offered yet is not behind, and an archived device follows no
// schedule. UNASSIGNED IS A FILTER TOO: `unassigned=true` answers the devices nobody holds.
//
// WHERE A DEVICE IS IN ITS LIFE IS A FILTER TOO (ONPLO-2802). `lifecycle_state=lost` is how
// the Overview finds its lost-mode cards without reading every page, and it is the list the
// Overview's last card opens when more than 100 devices are lost. `retired` and `recycled`
// are in the archive, so they are answered beside `retired=true` alone. Asked without it, the
// call is refused (422 naming `lifecycle_state`), and so is a state in use asked of the
// archive.
//
// TWO ORDERS (ONPLO-2802). `worst_first`, the default, is the work queue above.
// `newest_first` is the Overview's Recently added, as the spec lane ruled it (ONPLO-2917):
// the device that joined the register last comes first, by `created_at`, and of two that
// joined in one moment the one with the greater id. An enrolment that has not finished is no
// device yet, so it is in neither order. A cursor carries the order it was minted in, and the
// other order refuses it.
//
// TO RECLAIM IS A FILTER TOO (ONPLO-3238): `to_reclaim=true` answers a leaver's company
// devices, those whose `reclaim` is open, in the order asked for. It is how Company devices
// finds its To reclaim section, one of the "Sections that appear only when they hold something".
//
// A RETIRED DEVICE IS ARCHIVED, AND THE LIST LEAVES IT OUT (ONPLO-2674): "Retiring archives the
// record — hidden from the default list, evidence and history kept". So does a recycled one,
// which was retired first. `retired=true` is the page's Show retired, and answers the archive:
// those devices alone, in the same order, read and judged as every other row.
//
// EACH DEVICE IS ASKED ABOUT, NEVER THE WHOLE REGISTER. Seeing devices can be given on one
// device, or on a group, which reaches every device in it. So the list asks no permission
// of the account environment as a whole: each row is judged by `devices:device.read`, and
// somebody given one laptop is answered that laptop.
//
// THE GROUP A QUERY NAMES IS ASKED ABOUT TOO (decision 0782): `devices:group.read`, of that
// one group, before the list is read. A group you may not see answers an empty list.
//
// Corresponds with GET /devices (the `ListDevices` operationId).
func (c *Client) ListDevices(ctx context.Context, params *ListDevicesParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewListDevicesRequest(c.Server, params)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

// CreateDeviceWithBody Add a device by hand
//
// THE BY-HAND DOOR: "a record for something that can report nothing at all". Nothing is
// installed and nothing is reached. The record exists so the thing has a name, an owner and
// a place in the list.
//
// THIS COMPUTER AND A SERVER JOIN THROUGH `startEnrolment` INSTEAD: each ends in a device
// that reports on itself. The remaining doors are not declared yet.
//
// A BY-HAND RECORD READS `unknown`, AND STAYS SO. Unknown means the device exists and
// nothing has ever reported. Its reading names its source as `by_hand`.
//
// THE OWNERSHIP KIND IS SAID, NEVER GUESSED. It fixes what may be collected from a device and
// which actions exist, so the request states it. A personal environment has no company:
// `company_owned` and `shared` are refused there (422 naming the field). A device of your own
// is made in every kind of environment.
//
// WHEN IDENTITY DID NOT SAY WHICH KIND OF ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT THE REQUEST IS IN, no device
// is made and the call answers 503 `authorization_unavailable` — ask again — and never the
// refusal above: not known is not personal. That holds for a device of your own too, since
// the answer names where the new device is in its life (`lifecycle_state`), and a device
// of your own that Nobody holds reads `assigned` in a personal account environment and
// `enrolled` in a company.
//
// WHO HOLDS IT IS YOU, OR NOBODY. Leave `assigned_to_person_id` out and the device is yours.
// Send `null` and it is Nobody's — a printer, a sensor, a screen in reception — and counts
// toward nobody's readiness. Naming another person is refused (422 naming the field):
// handing a device to somebody is its own act with its own permission, and is not declared
// here.
//
// WHO MAY BIND A DEVICE TO NOBODY is said on the Enrolment page, per door, and that page's
// configuration is not declared yet. Until it is, this operation's permission is the whole
// gate.
//
// THE ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT'S FIRST DEVICE BRINGS ITS THREE UPDATE SCHEDULES, in the same act:
// Test devices, Everyone and Servers.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with POST /devices (the `CreateDevice` operationId).
