// RemoveWorkData Remove work data from a person's own device
//
// REMOVE WORK DATA FROM A PERSON'S OWN DEVICE: "remove work data only from a person's own
// device, leaving everything that is theirs" (the permission catalogue's row). The answer is
// the `remove_work_data` action as it starts. Read it again with `getDeviceAction` to see it
// reach `done`. The device reads `retired` from that moment, not from this call.
//
// IT UN-ENROLS THE DEVICE. "A person's own device … is never wiped whole — it is un-enrolled
// and its work profile removed" (Devices → Ownership kinds). The work profile is what manages
// it, so taking it off ends what Onplo can do there (ONPLO-3718, decided by the lane on 29
// October 2026). So it goes as un-enrolling does (`unenrolDevice`):
//  1. this call records the action. Nothing is taken from the device yet;
//  2. the device is handed the action in the answer to its next check-in, takes the work
//     profile off with everything in it, and leaves the rest of the device as it is;
//  3. its next report says the action is `done` (`checkIn`). Its certificate is ended with
//     identity first, and then, in one step, the record reads `retired`, a repair or lost
//     mode it was in ends, and the receipt is kept with `work_data_removed` `true`
//     (`Device.retirement`).
//
// ONE THE DEVICE NEVER ANSWERS ends `expired` after the queued-action expiry, and the device
// stays as it was; one it answers `failed` leaves it as it was too. Either may be asked again.
//
// ONLY A PERSON'S OWN DEVICE ENROLLED WITH A COMPANY HOLDS WORK DATA. In a business account
