// CompleteEnrolmentWithCodeWithBody Finish enrolling a server, with its one-time code
//
// THE ONE OPERATION HERE THAT TAKES NO CREDENTIAL (decision 0644, rule 5). A server is
// enrolled by nobody signed in: "one install command and a one-time code". The code, in the
// body, is the whole proof. It was shown once to the person who started the enrolment, it
// works once, and it binds one device to the account environment that enrolment was started
// in.
//
// IT FINISHES THAT ONE ENROLMENT AND DOES NOTHING ELSE. It reads no record, reaches no other
// device and signs nobody in. What comes back is the new device's id and its certificate —
// nothing about the person who started it, and nothing about the account environment.
//
// THE CODE TRAVELS IN THE BODY, never in the path or a query string: a URL is written to logs
// a body is not. And the address carries no id, so a caller who does not hold the code
// learns nothing from it.
//
// WHAT HOLDS GUESSING. The code is long — at least thirty-two characters — because it is
// pasted beside the install command and never typed from memory; a code that long is not
// found by trying. It stops 24 hours after it was shown. And every way a code can fail
// answers the SAME 404: unknown, already used, replaced by a new one, past its 24 hours, or
// cleared with its enrolment. A caller learns nothing from which. A door with no credential
// is limited (decision 0732), and a caller who calls too often is answered 429.
//
// A CODE IS SPENT ONLY BY SUCCEEDING. A certificate request that is refused — the 422s
// `completeEnrolment` lists — leaves the code good, so the server may try again with a
// request that is right.
//
// NO 401 AND NO 403: it never asks who is calling, so it can refuse nobody for who they are.
// An `Authorization` header sent anyway is not read.
//
// A 503 MEANS IDENTITY COULD NOT BE REACHED, and nothing about the caller (decision 0821). The
// finish tells identity the device exists and then asks it for the device's certificate; when
// either cannot be done, `identity_unavailable` answers, nothing was enrolled, and the code is
// still good, so the same request may be sent again. It carries no `Retry-After`, which only
// the 429 does (decision 0732). (Until ONPLO-2392 this said "no 503", and an identity outage
// would have reached the caller as a 500.)
//
// **NOT SERVED YET — it answers 501 until a server's enrolment can be started.** No code
// exists before then: the answer that shows one carries the install command, which names
// the device agent's address and release key, and neither exists yet (ONPLO-2329). The
// service behind it is built (ONPLO-1078).
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with POST /enrolment-completions (the `CompleteEnrolmentWithCode` operationId).
func (c *Client) CompleteEnrolmentWithCodeWithBody(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewCompleteEnrolmentWithCodeRequestWithBody(c.Server, contentType, body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

