// CountDevicesWithResponse How many devices you may see, by kind, by health word and by state
//
// THE OVERVIEW'S NUMBERS, COUNTED BY DEVICES ITSELF (ONPLO-2762): how many devices of each
// kind, how many read each of the five health words, how many stand at each state of their
// life, and how many are past an update's deadline. One call, whatever the size of the fleet.
//
// THE COUNT IS THE LIST COUNTED. Every number is what reading every page of `listDevices`
// with no filter and tallying the rows would give, at the same moment: the same devices, each
// word the one its row reads, an exempt device under `exempt` and never under the word it
// reads of its own. So a count never shows a device the list would hide, and never leaves out
// one the list would show.
//
// THE ARCHIVE IS COUNTED APART (ONPLO-2674). A retired or recycled device is archived and left
// out of the list with no filter, so it is left out of every number above too: the fleet is
// the devices in use. `retired_count` and `recycled_count` alone count the archive, what
// `listDevices` with `retired=true` would answer, so the five other states add up to
// `device_count` and those two stand outside it. An archived device's health word is not
// counted, and Compliance is not asked about it.
//
// EACH DEVICE IS ASKED ABOUT, AS ON THE LIST. Seeing devices can be given on one device, or on
// a group, which reaches every device in it. A count has no rows the door could judge, so
// Devices judges them: it asks identity which of the account environment's devices you may
// see, a hundred at a time, and counts those. Somebody given one laptop counts one. A grant on
// a group counts the devices in it.
//
// A DEVICE PAST AN UPDATE'S DEADLINE is counted as a schedule's row counts it, by its worst
// update: one whose update is failing is failing, never past its deadline as well. So for
// somebody who may see every device, `past_update_deadline_count` is the sum of
// `devices_past_deadline_count` over the rows of `listUpdateSchedules`. It is counted here and
// not read from the schedules because a schedule's counts are of every device that follows it,
// and seeing schedules is granted across the account environment alone.
//
// A COUNT ANSWERED IS A LOOK (decision 0675): one event that names no device.
//
// IT ANSWERS IN THE ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT THIS CREDENTIAL ACTS IN and never beyond it. WHEN
// IDENTITY DID NOT SAY WHICH KIND THIS IS, it answers 503 `authorization_unavailable`, as the
// list does: every state is named by the kind.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /device-counts (the `CountDevices` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) CountDevicesWithResponse(ctx context.Context, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*CountDevicesResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.CountDevices(ctx, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseCountDevicesResponse(rsp)
}

