// A SELECTION IS UP TO 200 DEVICES BY ID, each named once. A group is sent as the devices in it,
// read with `listDevices` narrowed to the group; more than 200 are sent in parts, each asked on
// its own.
//
// "EACH ONE ASKS ONCE, NAMES HOW MANY DEVICES IT WILL TOUCH" (Business environment → Devices app
// → Company devices). The devices it touches are those named that you may act on, that are in
// this account environment and that something reaches. Until `expected_device_count` says
// exactly that number, the call is refused `device_count_to_confirm`, `detail` says the number,
// and nothing is written. When none would be touched, nothing is asked and the answer records
// nothing.
//
// EACH DEVICE IS ASKED ITS OWN PERMISSION, AND NOTHING ACROSS THE ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT (decision
// 0808). Holding it on a device, or on a group the device is in, is enough for that device: a
// person given it on three devices acts on those three. A device you may not act on is never
// skipped in silence: it is in `not_reached` as `device_not_found`, exactly as one that does not
// exist, or is another environment's. A record made by hand, or a retired device, is there as
// `nothing_to_reach`.
//
// ONE ACTION PER DEVICE, each read again with `getDeviceAction`, and ONE AUDIT RECORD PER DEVICE
// naming who asked, written in the same transaction as the actions. Either all of them are
// recorded or none is.
//
// A BULK ACTION IS A JOB, AND THE JOB HAS A ROW (ONPLO-2597). A call that touches at least one
// device records one job, and each action it records names it (`bulk_action_id`). The job is
// read with `getBulkAction`, or with `getMyBulkAction` by whoever started it, with its count,
// done, waiting and failed. A call that touches none records no job.
//
// A GROUP SENT IN PARTS IS STILL ONE JOB (ONPLO-2729). The first part names the whole count in
// `whole_device_count`, and each later part names the job in `bulk_action_id`. The job takes parts
// until that count is reached, or until 10 minutes pass with no new part. A part from anyone but
// the job's starter, of another kind, past the count, naming a device already in the job, or after
// the job stopped taking parts is refused `not_a_part_of_the_job` and nothing is written; start a
// new job for what is left. A part that touches nothing leaves the job as it was. If the parts stop
// early, the job ends with what it received, and its `not_sent_count` says how many were never sent.
//
// WHEN NOTHING IN THE JOB IS WAITING ANY MORE, the device
// report that moved its last waiting action tells the person who started it, once, under
// `devices.bulk-action-finished`, with how many devices are done, waiting and failed. A job an
// agent or a machine started tells nobody. A job whose last waiting action ends by expiring, with
// no report after it, is told of by Devices' sweep within minutes of that expiry. (Until
// ONPLO-2597's change this read "The one notification when a job ends is not declared yet"; until
// ONPLO-3240's, that such a job "is not told of yet".)
//
// "TRY THE ONES THAT FAILED AGAIN" (Company devices) IS THIS CALL ONCE MORE, naming the job in
// `retry_of_bulk_action_id` and the devices that failed in it: the `device_id` of each outcome
// `getBulkAction` or `getMyBulkAction` answers `failed` or `expired`. It records a new job of
// them, which names the job it retries. Each device is still asked its own permission, and one
// you may not act on is in `not_reached` as before. Every other device named must have failed in
// that job, and `text` is the job's, as each of its actions'
// `message` says. A job that is not this account environment's, or not a message, or a device that
// did not fail in it, or another text, is refused `not_a_retry_of_the_job` before anything is
// counted, and nothing is written. The retry's count is asked and confirmed as any act's is.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /device-screen-messages (the `SendScreenMessageToDevices` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) SendScreenMessageToDevicesWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*SendScreenMessageToDevicesResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.SendScreenMessageToDevicesWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseSendScreenMessageToDevicesResponse(rsp)
}

