// CheckInWithBodyWithResponse A device reports on itself
//
// THE ONE OPERATION A DEVICE CALLS, AND ONLY ABOUT ITSELF (decision 0644, rule 4). The caller
// is the device named in the path, proved by the token its certificate was traded for. A
// person, a key, an agent and any other device are refused. No permission is evaluated: a
// device holds none, and "about itself" is a rule about whose record it is.
//
// IT IS ON THE PUBLIC API LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE. A customer's own software that holds a
// device's certificate may call it; Onplo's device agent is one caller of it, never the
// only one allowed.
//
// WHAT A REPORT CARRIES IS WHAT IS ALWAYS COLLECTED: the hardware and the operating system,
// whether the disk is encrypted and how full it is, and one reading per protection rule of
// the shipped setups — each "on, fixed" in the collection table. Nothing a person could
// switch off is in the report yet.
//
// HEALTH IS COMPUTED HERE, AT CHECK-IN, AND READ ON THE DEVICE'S RECORD — not in this answer.
// The word a person sees may be `exempt`, which is another service's to say, and a check-in
// waits on nobody: it is the most frequent call this API takes.
//
// ONE REPORT DOES WAIT, AND ONLY ONE: the one that says the device's `unenrol` action is
// `done` (ONPLO-2666). Its certificate is ended with identity before anything of the report
// is kept, and then the report is kept, the action finishes and the record reads `retired`,
// together. When identity cannot be reached, the call answers 503 `identity_unavailable` and
// nothing of the report is kept: send it again. When the report is kept, the device has
// made its last check-in, and the answer hands it nothing to do. A report from a retired
// device is refused 409 `device_is_retired`, and nothing of it is kept: one that sends its
// last report again, because the answer was lost, learns it is done. Its certificate is
// ended, so whatever it still holds to call with runs out.
//
// A device checks in on its interval and whenever something pushes it; the answer says how
// long it may wait before the next one.
//
// EACH REPORT IS KEPT, AND THE AUDIT LOG GETS THE CHANGE (decision 0726): Devices keeps the
// reading for "Health readings kept", and writes one audit event when the device's health
// word moved, naming the rules that moved it. A report that leaves the word as it was leaves
// none.
//
// THE DEVICE SAYS WHICH SETUP VERSION IT HOLDS (`setup_state`, ONPLO-2512): "Devices report
// back at the next check-in". What it said is kept, one per device, and read on the setup's
// Drift tab. A device that FAILED to apply the version it follows reads `not_compliant` with
// the failing rule `setup drift`, whatever the mode: "a device that stays in drift reads Not
// compliant · setup drift". A device that applied it and DRIFTED since is the mode's to
// settle: under `recommend` it reads the same failing rule — "Recommend applies once and
// reports drift as a failing rule" — and under `enforce` it is handed the version again
// (`FollowedSetup.apply_again`) — "Enforce re-applies whenever a device drifts".
//
// THE DEVICE IS HANDED THE SOFTWARE IT IS TO INSTALL, AND SAYS HOW IT WENT (ONPLO-2703). "A
// required item installs at the next check-in": the answer's `software` lists each Required
// item that reaches the device, a package by a download address Storage issues for this device
// in this answer, and the report's `software_outcomes` says `installed` or `failed` for each.
// The item's row counts installed, waiting and failed from those outcomes. Storage is the one
// service asked here, after the report is kept and outside its transaction, and only for a
// package: when it cannot be reached, the answer hands no package this time.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /devices/{device_id}/check-ins (the `CheckIn` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) CheckInWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deviceId DeviceId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*CheckInResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.CheckInWithBody(ctx, deviceId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseCheckInResponse(rsp)
}

