// LockDeviceWithResponse Lock a device
//
// LOCK A DEVICE NOW — "reversible, deleting nothing, and pushed immediately whatever the
// interval says" (the permission catalogue's row for this act). The answer is the action as it
// starts; read it again with `getDeviceAction` to see it reach `done`.
//
// UNDONE ON THE DEVICE, NOT HERE. The person at the device unlocks it with its own passcode, so
// no call unlocks a device. Lost mode is its own act and its own permission: `startLostMode`.
//
// PUSHED AT ONCE TO A DEVICE THAT IS ONLINE — ONCE A CHANNEL TO A DEVICE EXISTS (ONPLO-1869).
// Until then it is handed over in the answer to the device's next check-in, at most its
// interval away. One that is offline has not refused anything: the lock waits, `queued`,
// until it checks in, and ends `expired` after the queued-action expiry.
//
// IT MAY ASK YOU TO PROVE IT IS YOU. Settings → Action safeguards → "Re-ask the passkey" is on
// by default before a lock. While it is on, the call is refused `passkey_proof_required` unless
// this session proved itself with a passkey in the last five minutes, and one proof serves
// every re-ask in that window. What clears it is `startPasskeyProof` and then
// `completePasskeyProof` on the identity API, and then this call again. Whether the switch is on
// is `getActionSafeguards`'s `reask_passkey.lock` (ONPLO-2565); with it off, no proof is asked.
//
// A RECORD MADE BY HAND IS REFUSED `nothing_to_reach`, as for a restart: nothing reaches it.
// Nothing is recorded. So is a retired device (ONPLO-2666).
//
// ONE DEVICE, ITS OWN PERMISSION, ASKED OF THIS DEVICE. Holding it on the device, or on a group
// the device is in, is enough. ONE AUDIT RECORD naming who asked, written when the action is
// recorded. Many devices at once is `lockDevices` (ONPLO-2442).
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /devices/{device_id}/lock (the `LockDevice` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) LockDeviceWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deviceId DeviceId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*LockDeviceResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.LockDevice(ctx, deviceId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseLockDeviceResponse(rsp)
}

// EndLostModeWithResponse End lost mode
//
// THE DEVICE WAS FOUND. "If found: Lost mode → End, and the device unlocks with its own
// passcode." Switching it off "returns the device to the state it left" (decision 0761,
// point 1): `in_repair` for a device that was in repair, and otherwise `enrolled` or `assigned`
// as a device taken back from repair reads. The answer is that state, and the
// `lost_mode_end` action that takes the lost-mode screen off the device.
//
// WHICH STATE THAT IS, TODAY, is the one `takeDeviceBackFromRepair` names, for the same reasons.
// WHEN IDENTITY DID NOT SAY WHICH KIND OF ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT THIS IS, the call answers 503 and
// nothing is changed: not known is not personal. Ask again.
//
// ONLY FROM `lost`. A device that is not in lost mode is refused, and the refusal names the
// state it is in: `device_is_in_repair`, `device_is_enrolled`, `device_is_assigned` or
// `device_is_retired` — retiring a lost device ended its lost mode (ONPLO-2666). Nothing is
// changed or queued.
//
// NO PASSKEY IS RE-ASKED. Action safeguards re-ask it before the acts that hurt when they are
// wrong; ending lost mode unlocks nothing, because the person at the device still unlocks it
// with its own passcode.
//
// THE SAME PERMISSION AS SWITCHING IT ON, asked of this device: the catalogue's one row is "put
// a device in lost mode … and take it out again". ONE AUDIT RECORD naming who ended it.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with DELETE /devices/{device_id}/lost-mode (the `EndLostMode` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) EndLostModeWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deviceId DeviceId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*EndLostModeResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.EndLostMode(ctx, deviceId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseEndLostModeResponse(rsp)
}

// StartLostModeWithResponse Put a device in lost mode
//
// A LOST DEVICE IS LOCKED, AND THE RECORD READS LOST. "LOST switches lost mode on and allows
// locating." The answer is the device's place in its life, `lost`, and the action that tells
// the device; read the action again with `getDeviceAction` to see it reach `done`.
//
// FROM `enrolled`, `assigned` OR `in_repair` (decision 0761, point 1). A device already in lost
// mode is refused `device_is_lost`, and a retired one `device_is_retired` (ONPLO-2666);
// nothing is changed or queued. A device in repair keeps its repair while it is lost, and
// reads `in_repair` again when lost mode ends.
//
// WHAT THE DEVICE IS HANDED, TODAY: a `lost_mode` action, handed over in the answer to its
// next check-in until a channel to a device exists (ONPLO-1869), and `expired` after the
// queued-action expiry like any other action. The device stays lost whatever becomes of the
// action: the state is the record's.
//
// NOT YET, AND SAID SO. The page says the lock screen shows "message and callback", whose one
// home is Settings → Device defaults: that page is not declared, and its starting text names
// the account environment, which Devices cannot read (ONPLO-2441). So the device is handed no
// message, and the action's `message` is `null`. "Location on until it is found" waits on
// locating a device being declared.
//
// IT MAY ASK YOU TO PROVE IT IS YOU, as a lock does. Settings → Action safeguards → "Re-ask the
// passkey" is on by default "before lock, lost mode, wipe, …". While it is on, the call is
// refused `passkey_proof_required` unless this session proved itself with a passkey in the last
// five minutes. What clears it is `startPasskeyProof` and then `completePasskeyProof` on the
// identity API, and then this call again. Whether it is on is `getActionSafeguards`'s
// `reask_passkey.lost_mode` (ONPLO-2565).
//
// A RECORD MADE BY HAND IS REFUSED `nothing_to_reach`, as for a lock: nothing reaches it, so
// there is nothing to put in lost mode. Nothing is recorded.
//
// ONE DEVICE, ITS OWN PERMISSION, ASKED OF THIS DEVICE. Holding it on the device, or on a group
// the device is in, is enough. ONE AUDIT RECORD naming who asked.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /devices/{device_id}/lost-mode (the `StartLostMode` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) StartLostModeWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deviceId DeviceId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*StartLostModeResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.StartLostMode(ctx, deviceId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseStartLostModeResponse(rsp)
}

// GetManagedAndCompliantConditionWithResponse Whether a device is managed and compliant, and why not
//
// "THE MANAGED-AND-COMPLIANT CONDITION: one computed yes/no per device, published for other apps
// to read — this device is enrolled in THIS account environment, its health reads Compliant, and
// its last check-in is inside the environment's freshness window." Keys' company rule "Autofill
// only on managed devices" reads it (ONPLO-4644).
//
// A NO ALWAYS SAYS WHY, because "every place it is read shows WHY it currently reads no":
// `not_enrolled` — no device agent or management protocol reports it here (a record kept by
// hand, one another system manages, or one retired or recycled); otherwise the health word the
// device reads, `not_compliant` with the failing rules named, `unknown`, `stale` or `exempt`;
// and `check_in_too_old` — it reads Compliant and its last check-in is older than the freshness
// window. The first that holds, in that order.
//
// AN EXEMPT DEVICE READS NO. The condition asks for a health that "reads Compliant", and an
// exemption is its own word. Whether an exemption should count is the specification's open
// question (ONPLO-1018, point 11); until it says otherwise the word is answered as it reads.
//
// THE FRESHNESS WINDOW is the account environment's, set in Settings → Device defaults from 1 to
// 168 hours and 24 until changed (`DeviceDefaults.freshness_window_hours`, decision 0978): "An
// older reading is treated as unknown rather than as a pass." It is read each time the condition
// is asked, and the answer names the window it was judged against (ONPLO-4675).
//
// DEVICES PUBLISHES THE CONDITION; IT NEVER DECIDES WHAT ANOTHER APP DOES WITH IT. Nothing is
// written but the look, about whoever holds the device.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /devices/{device_id}/managed-and-compliant (the `GetManagedAndCompliantCondition` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) GetManagedAndCompliantConditionWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deviceId DeviceId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*GetManagedAndCompliantConditionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.GetManagedAndCompliantCondition(ctx, deviceId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseGetManagedAndCompliantConditionResponse(rsp)
}

// ReportMonitorReadingsWithBodyWithResponse A device sends what it measured for its monitors, every five minutes
//
// WHAT A DEVICE'S MONITORS MEASURE (ONPLO-3225). A device calls it about itself, as it calls
// `checkIn` (decision 0644, rule 4), and no permission is evaluated.
//
// WHAT IT READS IS WHAT IT WAS HANDED: the monitors of the setup version it follows, handed at
// every check-in (`FollowedSetup.monitors`). It reads each once a minute, and battery health and
// a certificate's days at each check-in, and sends what it read every five minutes, apart from
// the check-in (ONPLO-4970). A reading of something the version it follows does not watch is
// not kept, and is counted in the answer.
//
// THE SERVER JUDGES, NOT THE DEVICE. A monitor raises a FINDING when every reading for as long as
// it "holds for" is past its threshold, and the finding clears at the first reading back within
// it. One finding per monitor and device stands at a time. Raising one leaves one audit event,
// and clearing it another: "a monitor fired, silenced or recovered" is on the device's history.
//
// ONE ALERT FOR THE FINDING. "A monitor that crosses its threshold raises a FINDING, sends one
// alert for it, opens a task where the setup says so, and is a Flows trigger." The alert is the
// news `MonitorCrossedThreshold` (`api-contracts/devices/proto/monitor_events.proto`), written
// once for a finding. "A silence holds a monitor's finding and its alert for a maintenance
// period": a finding raised while a silence on the device or on one of its groups covers it is
// kept, and its alert waits until a reading comes after the silence ends with the finding still
// standing. A finding that clears while silenced sends none.
//
// A READING SENT TWICE IS ONE READING: a reading is kept by its minute, so a send whose answer
// was lost is sent again. A reading older than two days is not kept, because a reading is kept
// per minute for two days (ONPLO-4970), and is counted in the answer. One more than five minutes
// ahead of now is refused `reading_in_the_future`, and nothing of the send is kept.
//
// WHAT MONITORS MEASURE SWITCHED OFF (ONPLO-4971) hands the device no monitor, so every reading sent
// is counted not watched, and a finding standing on it clears.
//
// WHAT IS NOT HERE YET. The setups that ship
// carry their monitors since ONPLO-4972. Opening a task where a monitor says so waits on the Tasks
// service's call. Reading a finding back, on Device health and the device's Monitors tab, is
// not declared.
//
// A RETIRED DEVICE is refused 409 `device_is_retired`, as its check-in is, and nothing is kept.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /devices/{device_id}/monitor-readings (the `ReportMonitorReadings` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ReportMonitorReadingsWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deviceId DeviceId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ReportMonitorReadingsResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ReportMonitorReadingsWithBody(ctx, deviceId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseReportMonitorReadingsResponse(rsp)
}

