// StartEnrolmentWithBodyWithResponse Start enrolling this computer, or a server
//
// AN ENROLMENT IS STARTED BY A PERSON AND FINISHED BY SOMETHING ELSE. Starting says what the
// device will be called, whose the hardware is and who holds it. Finishing is the moment the
// device sends the public half of its own key and gets a certificate back.
//
// TWO DOORS ARE DECLARED. `this_device` — the computer the desktop app is running on; the
// same session finishes it, with `completeEnrolment`. `server` — a server nobody is signed
// in on; it is finished by a one-time code, with `completeEnrolmentWithCode`.
//
// A SERVER'S ANSWER CARRIES THE INSTALL COMMAND AND THE ONE-TIME CODE, ONCE. The command is
// the one line an engineer runs on the server to install the device agent. The code binds
// that server to this account environment and works once. It is shown in this answer and
// never again; `reissueEnrolmentCode` makes a new one.
//
// A SERVER'S PLATFORM IS CHOSEN HERE, because there is one command per platform:
// `server_platform`, required for `server` and refused for `this_device`, whose platform
// the device reports itself when it joins. Each is refused 422 naming the field.
//
// IT ASKS YOU TO PROVE IT IS YOU: "Enrolling requires the person's passkey, so a device is
// always bound to an Onplo identity or, by an Administrator, to Nobody." Refused
// `passkey_proof_required` until you do, and what clears it is two calls on the identity
// API — `startPasskeyProof`, then `completePasskeyProof` — and then this one again. A proof
// counts for the same five minutes as a re-asked passkey in Settings → Action safeguards, the
// one number Devices keeps for how recent a proof must be.
//
// WHO HOLDS IT IS YOU, OR NOBODY, as for a record made by hand. And who starts or finishes
// an enrolment is never taken as who holds the device (decision 0644).
//
// AN ENROLMENT NOBODY FINISHES IS CLEARED AFTER SEVEN DAYS. Nothing has to be cancelled.
//
// IN A BUSINESS ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT IT SAYS WHICH PRIVACY NOTICE THE PERSON READ: "the text shown
// on the device per ownership kind", read with `getPrivacyNotice`. `privacy_notice_version` is
// that version of the enrolment's ownership kind, and the enrolment keeps it. Left out there, it is
// refused 422 (`privacy_notice_version_required`). A version that is not the latest is refused
// `privacy_notice_not_the_latest`, and `detail` says to read it again: the notice changed after the
// person read it, so they read it again before anything starts. A personal account environment has
// no notice, and a version sent there is refused 422 (`privacy_notice_version_not_allowed`). What a
// server's enrolment says of it arrives with the server door (ONPLO-2329).
//
// IN A BUSINESS ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT IT PASSES A DOOR, and a shut one refuses it
// (`enrolment_door_shut`, and `detail` names the door). This computer is the finishing step
// through a door, never a door of its own: your own device passes the Your own device door, and a
// company-owned or shared computer the by-hand door — which ships open and Administrator-only, so
// "enrolling a company-owned device is an Administrator act". Somebody the door is not for is refused
// `enrolment_door_not_for_you`: named on it, holding one of its roles or in one of its teams
// passes. A personal account environment has no doors. A server passes the server door
// once its enrolment is built (ONPLO-2329).
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /enrolments (the `StartEnrolment` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) StartEnrolmentWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*StartEnrolmentResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.StartEnrolmentWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseStartEnrolmentResponse(rsp)
}

