// UpdateSetupWithBodyWithResponse Change a setup
//
// ONLY WHAT IS SENT CHANGES, AND EVERY SAVE IS A VERSION. "Every save makes the next numbered
// version, and saving applies nothing." The answer carries the new `version_number`.
//
// NOTHING ON ANY DEVICE MOVES. A device is held to the version that was applied to it, and
// applying is its own act with its own permission (`applySetup`). So a rule switched off
// here is switched off in the setup's latest version and on no device until it is applied.
//
// A SETUP THAT SHIPS CAN BE CHANGED like any other: "only edited", never deleted.
//
// A RULE IS SENT WHOLE. Changing the minutes of the screen lock sends its switch and its
// requirement beside them. So is the staged rollout: both numbers, or `null` to turn it off.
// And so are the monitors, as the whole list the setup holds after the change.
//
// A VERSION IS NEVER TYPED AND NEVER REWRITTEN. "Every save makes the next numbered version,
// starting at 1." "Rolling back saves an older version's content as the next number, so
// numbers only grow and no version is ever rewritten." Rolling back is not an operation here
// yet: it picks from the versions, which are not declared (this file's header).
//
// ITS KINDS ARE CHANGED HERE TOO (decision 0800 point 3). "Adding a kind is always allowed.
// Taking one out is refused while a device of that kind follows the setup, naming how many,
// the same way deleting is" (409 `setup_is_followed`). Who follows it is `following_device_count`
// counts: so taking `computer` out of the Computer setup is refused while a computer
// nothing else is applied to is in the account environment. A kind taken out that no device
// follows the setup as comes out, and so does the setup's application to that kind. A change
// of kinds is a save, so it is the next version.
//
// HOW MANY IS IN `detail`, as a sentence to show: "2 devices follow this setup." The title
// says what to do.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
