// ApplySetupWithResponse Apply a version of a setup
//
// "APPLYING A SETUP IS DEPLOYING A NAMED VERSION." The version is named by its number, and it
// goes to the kinds and the single devices sent. "Every save makes the next numbered version,
// and saving applies nothing": this is the act that puts one on devices.
//
// ONE SETUP PER KIND AND PER DEVICE. A kind or a device this setup, or another, was applied to
// before is moved here: "every device follows exactly one". The narrowest wins — a single
// device, then a kind — so applying to a kind moves no device another setup is applied to by
// itself.
//
// A CHANGE TOUCHING MORE THAN TEN DEVICES PREVIEWS THE COUNT FIRST. When the devices that
// would follow this version afterwards, and do not follow it now, are more than ten, the call
// is refused `device_count_to_confirm` unless `expected_device_count` says exactly how many.
// The refusal's `detail` says the count as a sentence to show, and nothing is written. Send
// it again with that number. Ten or fewer need no count.
//
// ONLY A KIND THE SETUP FITS. A kind not among the setup's kinds, or a device whose kind is
// not, is refused `setup_does_not_fit`: "Which kinds of device may follow this setup."
//
// A SETUP THAT IS ITS MAKER'S OWN "applies only to devices they hold": to a single device
// assigned to the caller, and never to a kind, which reaches devices that are not theirs. One
// that does not is refused `setup_is_its_makers_own`. Promote it first (`promoteSetup`).
//
// A RETIRED DEVICE IS GIVEN NO SETUP. One named is refused `device_is_retired`, and nothing is
// written, for the other devices named either. A recycled device, which was retired first, is
// refused the same. Applying to a kind is never refused for a retired device of that kind.
//
// WHAT A DEVICE IS HANDED. At its next check-in the device is handed the version it follows
// and whether it is enforced (`CheckInReceipt.setup`). "Enforce re-applies whenever a device
// drifts. Recommend applies once." Its health word is judged from that version's Required
// rules from the first report after it, and a reading made before is not judged again.
//
// A DEVICE IS ASKED ABOUT ONE BY ONE. Seeing a device can be given on one device alone, so the
// door asks which of the devices named you may see (`devices:device.read`, decision 0752), and
// one you may not see answers exactly as one that does not exist.
//
// WHETHER IT TOOK is the device's to say, at its next check-in (`CheckInReport.setup_state`),
// and reads on the setup's Drift tab (`listSetupDrift`) and in its `drift_device_count`.
//
// A VERSION WHOSE STAGED ROLLOUT IS ON GOES TO ITS FIRST WAVE ONLY, AND THE REST KEEP WHAT THEY
// FOLLOWED until `rest_at` (`SetupRollout`). Only a device changing version waits. Applying
// something else that reaches a waiting device ends its wait. `listSetupRolloutDevices` says who
// is in which wave. A ROLLOUT WHOSE FIRST WAVE FAILS PAUSES (`StagedWaves.pause_threshold_percent`,
// `Setup.paused_rollouts`), and its rest keep waiting. A VERSION THAT SENDS THE TEST GROUP FIRST
// (`Setup.test_group_first`) gives the devices in it the version now, and the rest the Test group's
// lead time later; with no group marked it goes by its percentage and says so.
//
// Groups have an operation of their own, `applySetupToGroups`.
//
// ONE AUDIT EVENT PER KIND AND PER DEVICE applied to, naming the setup it followed before.
//
// Takes a body of the `application/json` content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /setups/{setup_id}/applications (the `ApplySetup` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ApplySetupWithResponse(ctx context.Context, setupId SetupId, body ApplySetupJSONRequestBody, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ApplySetupResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ApplySetup(ctx, setupId, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseApplySetupResponse(rsp)
}

// ListSetupDriftWithResponse Which devices hold a setup's version
//
// "DRIFT: which devices are applied, waiting or failed, with the reason." One row per device
// that follows this setup now, each with the version it follows, whether it is enforced, and
// where it stands: `applied`, `waiting` or `failed` (`SetupDriftState`). In the order the
// devices were added.
//
// WHERE A DEVICE STANDS IS READ FROM WHAT IT LAST SAID (`CheckInReport.setup_state`), against
// the version it follows now. So a version applied after a device's last report reads
// `waiting` for it until the device checks in again: "Devices report back at the next
// check-in". A device that has never said which version it holds reads `waiting` too.
//
// A DEVICE IS IN DRIFT when it reads `waiting` or `failed`, and the setup's
// `drift_device_count` counts exactly those. "Every other device applies at its next
// check-in; the Drift tab lists stragglers with the reason."
//
// WHO FOLLOWS IS `following_device_count`'S RULE: "the narrowest wins", and a device nothing
// is applied to follows the setup that ships for its kind. A hidden device follows nothing.
//
// ASKED AS THE SETUP IS, AT THE DOOR, as what it is applied to is (`listSetupApplications`):
// a row is where a setup stands on a device, read under the setup's own read, and it names
// the device by its id alone. Devices you may not see are among the rows, as they are among
// the counts. A setup kept to somebody else answers as one that does not exist.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /setups/{setup_id}/drift (the `ListSetupDrift` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ListSetupDriftWithResponse(ctx context.Context, setupId SetupId, params *ListSetupDriftParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ListSetupDriftResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ListSetupDrift(ctx, setupId, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseListSetupDriftResponse(rsp)
}

// ApplySetupToGroupsWithBodyWithResponse Apply a version of a setup to groups
//
// "THE NARROWEST WINS: a single device, then a group, then a kind." This puts a numbered version
// on the groups sent, between the two. Its own operation beside `applySetup`, because one
// operation asks one permission of the ids it names, and that one asks it of devices.
//
// ONE SETUP PER GROUP. A group another setup was applied to is moved here.
//
// "TWO GROUPS AT THE SAME LEVEL THAT NAME ONE DEVICE WITH DIFFERENT SETUPS ARE A CONFLICT.
// APPLYING IS REFUSED WHILE IT IS KNOWN, WITH THE DEVICES NAMED": a device in a group sent that
// is also in a group a different setup is applied to, with nothing applied to it by itself, is
// `setup_conflicts_with_another_group`, and `detail` names the devices by id. A device applied
// to by itself is not one: its own setup wins either way.
//
// A DEVICE OF A KIND THE SETUP DOES NOT FIT IS NOT REACHED THROUGH THE GROUP: "Which kinds of
// device may follow this setup." It follows what it followed, and no conflict is counted for it.
//
// A SETUP THAT IS ITS MAKER'S OWN GOES TO NO GROUP, as to no kind: a group reaches devices that
// are not theirs. `setup_is_its_makers_own`.
//
// THE COUNT, AS FOR `applySetup`: more than ten devices moved is `device_count_to_confirm`
// unless `expected_device_count` says how many.
//
// A GROUP IS ASKED ABOUT ONE BY ONE (`devices:group.read`, decision 0752), and one you may not
// see answers exactly as one that does not exist. ONE AUDIT EVENT PER GROUP applied to, naming
// the setup it followed before.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /setups/{setup_id}/group-applications (the `ApplySetupToGroups` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ApplySetupToGroupsWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, setupId SetupId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ApplySetupToGroupsResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ApplySetupToGroupsWithBody(ctx, setupId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseApplySetupToGroupsResponse(rsp)
}

